WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.840
Hello Again!
As you can hear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:03.840
I have not been fired by Letitia YET.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:06.480
Let's have a look at some more community questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:10.000
that were either interesting or caused&nbsp;
some confusion within the answers.

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:15.440
So, for the first question, opinions were&nbsp;
divided: most of you actually think the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:20.640
gradients of individual pixels show you something&nbsp;
useful (gradients regarding the output, that is).

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:23.120
What was meant here was simple:&nbsp;

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:26.800
Take for example a classifier that&nbsp;
gets an image and predicts a cat.&nbsp;

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:29.680
We now want to know: what&nbsp;
does the network look at?

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:34.080
For that, we simply get the gradient of each&nbsp;
individual pixel regarding the output, that is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:38.640
we see how much the output changes if we&nbsp;
slightly change a single pixel's value.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.320
In theory, that may sound great, but&nbsp;
the information of a single pixel can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:47.120
somewhat deceiving - a simple argument against&nbsp;
something like that, at least in our opinion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:49.520
would be for example what adversarial attacks do.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:52.800
There, you also look at a&nbsp;
pixel and see how you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.480
to change those pixels to have the&nbsp;
network predict a different class.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:01.600
As a result, a really tiny disturbances can&nbsp;
already cause a totally different classification.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:05.840
Simple pixel information seems to be&nbsp;
very unstable and not super reliable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.640
at least for many cases where I&nbsp;
personally have looked at that.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:12.880
And there are actual papers that look at that too&nbsp;
- these examples here compute simple gradients&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:17.920
for each pixel regarding the output class that&nbsp;
has been produced, showing what's "important"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:22.000
to produce this label for the image.
 
As you can see, this is super noisy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.920
and often pixels on the object itself&nbsp;
are not even the most important ones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.880
like for example for the camera, the lens is&nbsp;
actually not important in this visualization.

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:35.200
A more refined solution is what they suggest&nbsp;
instead: compute the gradient not just for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:39.840
one single pixel and its current color, but&nbsp;
integrate over the gradient of differently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.240
blended versions of the image, for example&nbsp;
blending from black to the actual image.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:49.200
And that then gets rid of some of the noise&nbsp;
and produces something that is more stable.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:54.160
For example if we now look at the camera again,&nbsp;
we can now see that the lens actually seems to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:57.360
help in the classification and&nbsp;
seems to be important for that.
 &nbsp;

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:00.480
If you want to know more about this,&nbsp;
you can find the integrated gradients&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:03.440
paper [https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.01365] and a&nbsp;
great distill article with more on that topic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:04.960
[https://distill.pub/2020/attribution-baselines/]&nbsp;
in the description box below

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:07.920
[Interpretability 2: Concepts]
Similar to the last question, this one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:11.920
also aims at better understanding what a neural&nbsp;
network actually does internally internally.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:14.640
Consider for example a&nbsp;
classifier for images again.&nbsp;

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:19.760
We then look at our examples and split them in&nbsp;
two different groups by some characteristics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:22.640
and we do that splitting in the&nbsp;
deeper layers of the network.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:26.960
For example, we split the dataset in images&nbsp;
with, and without water in the background.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:30.960
Now, the normal of that hyperplane that&nbsp;
splits our data in the latent space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:35.920
is now aactually a direction in which we can move&nbsp;
an image representation, for example in this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:40.560
to make it more or less "watery", in the&nbsp;
sense of what the network actually perceives.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:45.440
And we can now use that to measure how important&nbsp;
that water is for a classification result:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:49.680
We can compute the gradient of this direction&nbsp;
with respect to the output of the network.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:54.160
Through that we see how the output of the&nbsp;
classifier changes if we move a sample in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:58.640
the latent space along that hyperplane normal,&nbsp;
that is, how the output changes if we make an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:03.120
image more or less watery. For example for the&nbsp;
ship class, we should see that making an image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.760
more or less watery, should have an impact on&nbsp;
the classifier output, as I've been told that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:11.920
ships usually are in water and thus, that's&nbsp;
a good cue to be used for the class label.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:16.080
And coming back to the initial question: We&nbsp;
can also measure that way how important for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:19.440
example a text label is in an image&nbsp;
for the resulting classification.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.560
This is great to for example see if your&nbsp;
network just "cheats" on a task by abusing some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:27.440
(for example) text labels that&nbsp;
have been hard-coded in the image.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:32.640
A paper that looks at all of that in depth, called&nbsp;
TCAV, and that tries to measure that, quantify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:36.240
that in some way, is linked below. It's a really&nbsp;
interesting and well written paper that gives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:42.160
you a lot of new ways to view at what networks&nbsp;
internally and how we can put that in numbers.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:44.480
[VQ-VAE: Without Quantisation]
We then had two questions about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:49.040
Vector-Quantized Variational Autoencoders.&nbsp;
While those don't sound that exciting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:51.920
remember that they enable for&nbsp;
a great things like DALL-E.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:57.840
In principle, a VQ-VAE is an Autoencoder&nbsp;
where the latent space is quantized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:02.880
that is, you encode some input, then round the&nbsp;
latent codes to the closest codebook value,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:07.600
and then decode that discretized value,&nbsp;
to obtain the original input again.

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:11.680
This learned discrete representation then works&nbsp;
a bit better for some tasks because you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:16.560
more easily use, for example, transformers&nbsp;
on it that are built for discrete inputs.

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:20.000
Also, some things in the real world&nbsp;
like words or hand-written digits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:24.640
are just discrete, so it comes more naturally to&nbsp;
express them through a discrete representation.

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:28.800
Now, this codebook that we are using&nbsp;
to discretize the encoder output&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:32.000
is also learned during training, which&nbsp;
means that for the loss function,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:36.160
we now have the original reconstruction loss,&nbsp;
just as for a normal boring autoencoder,

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:41.040
and two parts the regularize the latent space&nbsp;
to better fit the values of the codebook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:44.240
while also moving the used codebook&nbsp;
items closer to the samples.

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:47.440
If we now just drop that vector quantization part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:51.280
namely, the two extra losses that adapt the&nbsp;
codebook to the latent codes and the latent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:55.280
codes to the codebook, all that's left&nbsp;
is the autoencoder reconstruction loss.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:05:00.320
And without that discretization - well,&nbsp;
we then just have a plain old autoencoder.&nbsp;

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:04.240
And that also means that we no longer get&nbsp;
the properties similar to a variational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:08.131
autoencoder (VAE), namely, a nice and&nbsp;
tidy latent through this codebook.

00:05:08.131 --> 00:05:10.720
[VQ-VAE: With infinite sized codebook]
Similar to that question before, let's look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:14.560
at what happens if we make the&nbsp;
codebook of a VQ-VAE infinitely large.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:18.160
For the loss function, remember&nbsp;
that we take our encoded image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:22.400
and then quantize that to the nearest&nbsp;
codebook values before we decode that.

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:24.560
Now there are two possible interpretations of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:28.160
question that largely depend on&nbsp;
the initial distribution of the,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:32.640
let's assume 1-dimensional here, codebook&nbsp;
and the initial distribution of the latent:

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:35.440
First of all, if we say that an&nbsp;
infinite codebook would actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:38.480
mean a codebook with all possible codewords in it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:42.480
then we would have all the possible numbers&nbsp;
our encoder could come up with in there.

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:47.280
If that's the case, then both these losses&nbsp;
would become zero - if every latent part is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:50.560
actually in the codebook, then we would&nbsp;
not need to move our codebook closer to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:54.640
the encoder output OR move the output&nbsp;
of the encoder closer to the codebook.

00:05:54.640 --> 00:06:00.080
And as a result, these terms are always zero, and&nbsp;
we just have a regular, old boring Autoencoder.

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:03.120
And that is was what I was actually&nbsp;
going for with this question.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:06.800
But as the comments pointed out, that's&nbsp;
not the only possibilie interpretation.

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:10.480
For the other possible interpretation,&nbsp;
let's say that our infinite codebook&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:14.720
has a certain distribution, for&nbsp;
example having numbers from [-1, 1].

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:18.720
If our encoder, luckily, falls&nbsp;
into that range and we stay there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:23.200
great, we're just not going to have any&nbsp;
quantization related losses that are non-zero.

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:28.080
BUT if we produce an encoding that gets out&nbsp;
of that range, things get a little bit hairy.

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:33.920
If we'd have a value outside of that range, say,&nbsp;
smaller than -1, we move that value that comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:40.240
out of the encoder towards -1 while moving the&nbsp;
codebook entry with -1 towards that actual value.

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:44.880
However, we JUST move that one entry on the&nbsp;
boundary there, not touching any other values&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:50.160
in the codebook for now, as the codebook is only&nbsp;
adapted for the codewords that are actually used.

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:55.600
So now, we have a codebook that has some values&nbsp;
being quantized and some not being quantized. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.600 --> 00:07:00.320
over the iterations, that will spread out, and&nbsp;
we'd for example end with something like this:

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:05.440
We have a core dense region between [-1, 1] where&nbsp;
quantization doesn't do anything because all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:09.920
values are in the codebook, and then there's&nbsp;
a region where we just regularly discretize.

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:16.560
So, this will eventually act like a regular&nbsp;
autoencoder for anything inside [-1, 1] and like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:22.560
regular VQ-VAE outside of that, which is somewhat&nbsp;
weird. Can you come up with a useful scenario for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:27.200
that? Or do you even think we overlooked something&nbsp;
and we get an entirely different behavior? If so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:30.800
hit us up in the comments, because for now,&nbsp;
that seems more like a pile of rubbish to me.

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:33.600
[Disentangling with Pearson's]
Next up is a FANTASTIC question (yay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:34.400
thank you for that!).

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:36.640
We wanted to know whether one could use Pearson's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:40.560
correlation coefficient to disentangle&nbsp;
the latent space of a neural network.

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:44.400
Well, disentangling basically means that&nbsp;
we want the latent of our neural network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:48.960
to be nice and tidy: For example that&nbsp;
of an autoencoder on handwritten digits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:53.840
is actually split into different characteristics,&nbsp;
like having one variable in the latent express&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:59.120
thickness and the other variable in the latent&nbsp;
expressing the number which is actually displayed.

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:03.840
Sadly, an unregularized latent space isn't that&nbsp;
cleaned up, and these characteristics are all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:08.720
over the place. Maybe we want a distribution&nbsp;
where the latent is nicely split into one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:13.280
variable having thickness and the other one&nbsp;
containing the digits, all nice and tidy.&nbsp;

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:15.840
But in truth, they're actually&nbsp;
looking like this instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:19.040
with one variable having both&nbsp;
characteristics in them a little bit.
 &nbsp;

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:26.720
And that's just one possible way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:30.560
but this is something we do not actually&nbsp;
want for disentangled representations.

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:34.880
What's important is that these variables&nbsp;
are somewhat correlated in latent space now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:38.400
meaning that for example when we have&nbsp;
some information about the X axis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:43.520
we also get you some information on the Y axis&nbsp;
as well. If we're in this part on the X axis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:46.800
we know that we can only be&nbsp;
in this part of the Y axis.

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:51.360
Throwing Pearson's correlation coefficient on&nbsp;
the loss to enforce that from X, we can no longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:56.960
linearly extrapolate Y would somewhat prevent that&nbsp;
- but there are a whole bunch of issues with that.

00:08:56.960 --> 00:09:00.640
We would just prevent a LINEAR correlation&nbsp;
that Pearson's correlation coefficient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:04.720
measures, but nothing more. Anything&nbsp;
non-linear is not really caught by that.&nbsp;

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:08.000
And as these networks are sneaky,&nbsp;
they can just choose to "hide" these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:12.960
properties in a non-linear fashion to circumvent&nbsp;
that, still being all messy and entangled.

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:16.240
[Point clouds]
And lastly, let's get to the juicy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:21.360
geometry. Regarding pointclouds, there's a whole&nbsp;
bunch of reasons why they're messy to work with.

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:25.120
From these answers, the number of points&nbsp;
is clearly not a problem - one could say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:30.640
that 128 points are actually all you need&nbsp;
to see that this is a chair. Opposing an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:38.160
MNIST image where you have 28-by-28 pixels&nbsp;
and you'd already end up with 784 values.

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:43.040
However, permutation invariance is a thing though&nbsp;
- you don't have a canonical order of those points&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:47.600
like you would have for images or voxels, that&nbsp;
are essentially just grids with values in them.&nbsp;

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:51.520
To explain one of the ideas to get around that&nbsp;
-- that was already mentioned in the comments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:54.960
thank your for that -- let's go quickly&nbsp;
over the idea of PointNet - you find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:57.600
the link to the well-written&nbsp;
paper in the description box.

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:02.480
The key idea is simple: put the points through a&nbsp;
function that is permutation invariant as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:08.640
For example, if we take the maximum of a, b and&nbsp;
c, that is the same as the maximum of b, c and a.

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:12.240
So, maximum is a symmetric function.&nbsp;
And that's what they're essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:16.640
using to become permutation invariant:&nbsp;
put all points through such a function.

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:21.360
What then they do, is they take every point&nbsp;
individually, apply a shared neural network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:26.800
to it to bloat it up to, say to 1000 dimensions,&nbsp;
and then apply max pooling across every dimension,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:30.800
ending up with 1000 maximum values that form&nbsp;
your embedding of the input point cloud.

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:35.600
So, by using this symmetric max function, we&nbsp;
got rid of the permutation sensitivity, while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:41.040
also not caring how many points we actually put in&nbsp;
here, because we always end up with 1000 values.

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:43.200
And this resulting embedding you can then process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:46.960
to for example classify with it whether&nbsp;
the input model is a plane or a chair.

00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:50.640
Aside from permutation invariance, you&nbsp;
have a bunch of other issues though:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:56.240
no properly defined convolution operation, varying&nbsp;
densities you'd get from a laser scanner, etc.

00:10:56.240 --> 00:11:00.640
And lots of fun with geometry! And that's&nbsp;
just the issues you have with pointclouds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:02.880
triangle meshes are even worse if you ask me ...!

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:05.600
So, thank you for staying with me&nbsp;
untill the end - if YOU have any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:09.920
cool suggestions for community questions,&nbsp;
just send them and we will credit you!

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:11.280
Hope to see you again!

